James inquired about constructing an interpolation in Julia for a set of \(x\) and \(y\) points that he could evaluate anywhere. I responded by suggesting the use of the `Interpolations.jl` package, providing a step-by-step guide. This included instructions for installing the package, creating a linear interpolation using example data points, and evaluating the interpolation at a new point. I also mentioned the possibility of choosing different interpolation methods, like cubic, for smoother results and emphasized the importance of sorted \(x\) values corresponding to their \(y\) values.
